<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Metztitlan</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1522 CE (approx.) · capital Metztitlan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland lordship in the Sierra Alta of Hidalgo that, like Tlaxcala, kept its independence from the Aztec Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Metztitlan arose in the fertile Vega de Metztitlan, a long valley north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, in the Postclassic period, probably by the 13th century. Its people were mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomí</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship was ruled from Metztitlan by a hereditary lord and controlled numerous towns in the rugged Sierra Alta. Its mountains and fortified frontier allowed it to repel Aztec campaigns, including one under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a> in the 1480s, and it remained an independent enclave, trading with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a>. Irrigated valley farming supported its population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, Metztitlan submitted to the Spanish around 1522. Augustinian friars built a large monastery there, and it became a colonial town.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1200 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:6.41%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1522 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd